1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,880
This is IntelliRoute: dynamic network routing, and multi-criteria path optimization.

2
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:10,084
We will build it with classical

3
00:00:10,084 --> 00:00:13,380
algorithms, graph theory, and mathematical optimization.

4
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:16,470
And we will understand it from scratch, so that

5
00:00:16,470 --> 00:00:19,410
when the professor asks, we can explain every line.

6
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:23,716
First: what is routing? When your computer sends a

7
00:00:23,716 --> 00:00:26,780
message to a server, it does not travel directly.

8
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:30,523
It hops through devices called routers. Each router receives

9
00:00:30,523 --> 00:00:33,270
the packet, and decides where to forward it next.

10
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:38,595
Three words to remember. A router forwards packets. A link connects

11
00:00:38,595 --> 00:00:41,990
two routers. A packet is a small unit of data.

12
00:00:42,370 --> 00:00:44,224
Think of routers as cities, links

13
00:00:44,224 --> 00:00:45,910
as roads, and packets as cars.

14
00:00:46,290 --> 00:00:48,930
Routing is simply deciding which roads the data should take.

15
00:00:50,110 --> 00:00:53,585
Now, the real problem. A packet must travel from router

16
00:00:53,585 --> 00:00:56,870
A to router F, and there are several possible paths.

17
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:00,274
The obvious choice is the shortest one. But the shortest

18
00:01:00,274 --> 00:01:03,190
route is not always the fastest, or the most reliable.

19
00:01:03,570 --> 00:01:06,523
One path has three links, but one of them

20
00:01:06,523 --> 00:01:10,483
is heavily congested. Another has four links, all fast.

21
00:01:10,483 --> 00:01:13,580
A third has only two, but it loses packets.

22
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:16,404
So which path is best? That depends

23
00:01:16,404 --> 00:01:18,360
on what we want to optimize.

24
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:23,367
Our research question: how can we dynamically select the most efficient

25
00:01:23,367 --> 00:01:28,580
route, using several performance parameters, instead of the shortest path alone?

26
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:33,125
Our foundation is Dijkstra's algorithm, developed

27
00:01:33,125 --> 00:01:35,940
by Edsger Dijkstra in nineteen fifty-six.

28
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:39,588
Its job is simple: find the path with the lowest

29
00:01:39,588 --> 00:01:43,060
total cost, between a start node and a destination.

30
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:47,908
Take this small network. Each number is the cost of crossing

31
00:01:47,908 --> 00:01:50,440
a link. We want to go from A to F.

32
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:55,209
Through B and D, the cost is eleven. Through B

33
00:01:55,209 --> 00:01:59,789
and E, fourteen. Through C and E, only seven. So

34
00:01:59,789 --> 00:02:02,460
the best path is A, C, E, F.

35
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,954
But Dijkstra does not list every path. It starts

36
00:02:05,954 --> 00:02:08,614
at A with distance zero, and looks at its

37
00:02:08,614 --> 00:02:11,080
neighbours: B costs four, C costs two.

38
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:15,172
Then it always picks the unvisited node with the smallest known

39
00:02:15,172 --> 00:02:19,120
cost, and updates its neighbours. This update is called relaxation.

40
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:21,665
The result is guaranteed optimal, as

41
00:02:21,665 --> 00:02:23,470
long as no weight is negative.

42
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:29,101
Here is the limitation. Suppose congestion raises the link

43
00:02:29,101 --> 00:02:31,710
from A to C, from two, to fifteen.

44
00:02:32,090 --> 00:02:34,415
The old route is no longer optimal. Dijkstra

45
00:02:34,415 --> 00:02:36,159
can find the new one, but only if

46
00:02:36,159 --> 00:02:38,590
someone updates the weights and runs it again.

47
00:02:38,970 --> 00:02:42,747
It does not monitor the network, and it does not decide

48
00:02:42,747 --> 00:02:46,180
how to model it. That is where our project begins.

49
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:50,576
Our idea: give every link a cost built from

50
00:02:50,576 --> 00:02:56,560
several measurements. Latency, packet loss, available bandwidth, and congestion.

51
00:02:56,940 --> 00:02:59,968
The weights of this formula express what we want to

52
00:02:59,968 --> 00:03:03,470
optimize, and the costs are refreshed as conditions change.

53
00:03:03,850 --> 00:03:06,465
To avoid switching routes too often, the system

54
00:03:06,465 --> 00:03:09,080
only changes path when the gain is significant.

55
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:13,168
What will we build? Not real internet

56
00:03:13,168 --> 00:03:16,940
routers. A network simulator, written in Python.

57
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:20,893
Part A: a topology editor, to create routers, connect

58
00:03:20,893 --> 00:03:23,860
them, and choose a source and a destination.

59
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:27,869
Part B: a condition simulator, to add congestion,

60
00:03:27,869 --> 00:03:31,350
latency, packet loss, and even router failures.

61
00:03:31,730 --> 00:03:36,745
Part C: the routing engine, with three strategies. Static Dijkstra,

62
00:03:36,745 --> 00:03:42,060
Dijkstra with updated weights, and our multi-criteria adaptive routing.

63
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:46,863
Part D: a results dashboard, showing the selected route, its

64
00:03:46,863 --> 00:03:51,360
cost, the latency, the delivery ratio, and the route changes.

65
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:54,439
How do we prove that it is

66
00:03:54,439 --> 00:03:57,800
better? With experiments, not with adjectives.

67
00:03:58,180 --> 00:04:00,478
We run one hundred simulated scenarios, and

68
00:04:00,478 --> 00:04:03,150
compare the algorithms under identical conditions.

69
00:04:03,530 --> 00:04:07,936
We measure average latency, packet delivery ratio, throughput,

70
00:04:07,936 --> 00:04:11,490
computation time, and the number of route changes.

71
00:04:11,870 --> 00:04:14,606
And we keep the comparison honest. We also

72
00:04:14,606 --> 00:04:18,188
test against Dijkstra using updated latency weights, to

73
00:04:18,188 --> 00:04:21,250
see whether multiple criteria really add value.

74
00:04:22,430 --> 00:04:24,751
We build this in six stages, instead of

75
00:04:24,751 --> 00:04:27,250
writing hundreds of lines of code at once.

76
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:32,996
Stage one: networking fundamentals. Stage two: classical algorithms,

77
00:04:32,996 --> 00:04:37,020
with Dijkstra, its complexity, and its limitations.

78
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,593
Stage three: the network cost model, where we design

79
00:04:40,593 --> 00:04:44,400
and normalize latency, congestion, bandwidth, and packet loss.

80
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:47,855
Stage four: dynamic routing, with automatic

81
00:04:47,855 --> 00:04:51,430
cost updates, route recalculation, and hysteresis.

82
00:04:51,810 --> 00:04:55,717
Stage five: simulation and interface. Stage six:

83
00:04:55,717 --> 00:04:59,950
experiments, where we compare, analyze, and improve.

84
00:05:00,330 --> 00:05:03,649
We must also be honest about the limits. Our weighted

85
00:05:03,649 --> 00:05:07,157
cost is a compromise. The chosen route is not guaranteed

86
00:05:07,157 --> 00:05:10,476
to be the fastest, the most reliable, and the highest

87
00:05:10,476 --> 00:05:14,610
bandwidth, all at once. We decide which trade-offs are acceptable.

88
00:05:14,990 --> 00:05:18,139
And our simulator demonstrates an algorithm. It will not

89
00:05:18,139 --> 00:05:21,120
change the routing tables of real internet providers.

90
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:26,620
Now, who does what? Three teammates, plus an AI assistant.

91
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,236
Member one leads the algorithms: the graph model,

92
00:05:30,236 --> 00:05:34,330
both Dijkstra baselines, and the multi-criteria cost function.

93
00:05:34,710 --> 00:05:37,476
Member two leads the simulator: the topology

94
00:05:37,476 --> 00:05:40,620
editor, the network conditions, and the dashboard.

95
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,165
Member three leads the experiments and the report:

96
00:05:44,165 --> 00:05:47,330
scenarios, metrics, plots, and the LaTeX document.

97
00:05:47,710 --> 00:05:52,165
The AI drafts code skeletons, tests, LaTeX, and diagrams, and

98
00:05:52,165 --> 00:05:55,597
it explains concepts. It is fast, but it can be

99
00:05:55,597 --> 00:05:59,540
confidently wrong, and it cannot see our real results.

100
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:02,298
So every AI output has a human owner, who

101
00:06:02,298 --> 00:06:04,850
checks it, and can explain it without notes.

102
00:06:05,230 --> 00:06:09,070
Humans own the research question, the experiments, and every final claim.

103
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:13,171
The final report is written in LaTeX, with the

104
00:06:13,171 --> 00:06:16,600
algorithms, the model, the experiments, and this plan.

105
00:06:16,980 --> 00:06:20,291
IntelliRoute. Not just the shortest path. The

106
00:06:20,291 --> 00:06:23,160
best one, for the conditions right now.
